INT. DECKER'S OFFICE - NIGHT

Cal reads Section Nine off his phone. Decker listens with his eyes closed, like a man hearing a song he doesn't like played well.

CAL
"Nothing in this act shall be construed as an admission of liability, successors and assigns, in any proceeding."

DECKER
Who wrote that?

CAL
The kid. Pruitt.

DECKER
The kid who reads the manual on the bus.

CAL
You know about the bus?

DECKER
Cal, everyone knows about the bus. He told the cafeteria lady about the bus.

He opens his eyes.

DECKER (CONT'D)
If Grace gets that clause, she votes yes.

CAL
We think.

DECKER
I know Grace. If she has cover, she votes her conscience. It's the most dangerous thing about her.

CAL
So we lose the vote.

DECKER
So there is no vote. Call Henry Osgood. I want a motion to suspend Rule 17.

CAL
That takes two-thirds.

DECKER
Then count me two-thirds.

CAL
Sir, the handshake--

DECKER
The handshake was for a vote on 212 as it stood at nine o'clock. That bill doesn't exist anymore. The kid rewrote it.
